WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.339
The Wall Street Journal recently ran a
story headlined, "The New Copycats: How

00:00:09.339 --> 00:00:13.870
Facebook Squashes Competition From
Startups". Basically, the tech giants can

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.410
buy out any startup they want or
aggressively imitate them and drive them

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:20.529
out of business.
How alarming should we find this? Well,

00:00:20.529 --> 00:00:24.279
judging by the growing number of voices
calling for regulating big tech, from

00:00:24.279 --> 00:00:28.210
Tucker Carlson to Elizabeth Warren, I
think I'm supposed to be pretty alarmed.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:32.950
But what is the public policy problem
here? Acquisition is typically a good

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:36.489
thing for the startups themselves, and
the startup sector is still pretty

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:41.140
vibrant. The main concern seems to be
whether the market dominance of these

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:45.370
tech giants is a bad thing. It's tough to
make that case from a consumer welfare

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:49.840
standpoint, since most of these products
are free. Plus, these companies are

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.590
certainly acting as if their dominance
is tenuous, given how closely they track

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:57.910
their potential competitors. In other
words, the sheer size of big tech

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:02.530
shouldn't concern us too much. Longer
term, it's their control of big data that

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:06.500
is likely to be where the antitrust
regulatory battle rages.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.220
To learn more about my take on regulating big tech,
